<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lahn is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 245 km long and a right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It rises in the Rothaar Mountains and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Giessen</a>, Wetzlar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a>, Limburg and Nassau to join the Rhine at Lahnstein. Its valley was a heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a> and other Hessian counties, and its iron ore supported mining and smelting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahn.jpg" alt="Lahn" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Verlaufskarte_Lahn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Lahn is about 245 km long, of which 23 km lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, 165.6 km in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and 57 km in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. It rises at the Lahnhof, near Netphen, in the south-eastern Rothaargebirge at 600 m, on the divide between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. It first runs north-east and east, then turns south at the Wetschaft and flows through the Marburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Gießen</a> valley and the Gießen Basin.</p><p>Below Wetzlar it narrows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a> valley, where the town of Weilburg lies in a loop of the river crossed by a boat tunnel unique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. After the Limburg Basin it enters the lower valley, cut more than 200 m deep into the Slate Mountains, and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at Lahnstein. Its longest tributaries are the Ohm and the Dill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The valley was settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, and a settlement of the Linear Pottery culture about 7,000 years old has been found at Dalheim near Wetzlar. The Roman fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-ems">Bad Ems</a> stood where the Limes crossed the Lahn, and the Waldgirmes Forum, found in the 1990s, was a Roman town that changed the understanding of Roman activity east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a> the Alamanni settled the lower valley and were later ousted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. The origin of the name is uncertain; it may be pre-Germanic. By the early 14th century the staple right of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Diez</a> points to considerable shipping, and by the end of the 19th century over 300 castles, fortresses and fortified churches had been built along the river.</p><p>Navigation was improved in stages, from John VI of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>'s towpath of 1559 to works by the Duchy of Nassau from 1808 and an agreement between Nassau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1816 to make the river passable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Gießen</a>. Iron ore mining around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a> raised the number of vessels counted at Runkel from 278 in 1827 to 464 in 1833, and Prussia wanted the ore for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>. Locks and the Weilburg ship tunnel were built by the 1840s. The Lahntal railway, built in 1857 to 1863, gradually drew freight away. Freight shipping ended in 1981.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Lahn is used only by recreational boats. Gravel mining in the valleys at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Gießen</a> from 1960 to 1996 left lakes and one of the largest nature reserves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, the Lahnau Nature Preserve. A cycle path runs the length of the valley, and wine is grown at Obernhof and Weinähr. Limburg Cathedral stands on an outcrop of Devonian limestone known as Lahn marble.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lahn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts of Laurenburg, who held lands on the <b>Lahn</b>, built Nassau Castle around 1125 on land claimed by the Bishopric of Worms.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau">County of Nassau</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1255 the counts of Nassau divided their lands along the <b>Lahn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-dillenburg">County of Nassau-Dillenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Walramian line of the house of Nassau held lands south of the <b>Lahn</b>, and a partition in 1344 gave Count John I a share centred on Weilburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-weilburg">County of Nassau-Weilburg</a></li><li><blockquote>One line took Braunfels castle above the <b>Lahn</b> as its seat and became the counts of Solms-Braunfels, ruling lands between Wetzlar and the Taunus, held directly from the Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-solms-braunfels">County of Solms-Braunfels</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1815, the Congress of Vienna added former Orange-Nassau lands, consolidating a state between the Rhine, Main and <b>Lahn</b>, with Wiesbaden as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a></li><li><blockquote>Wetzlar grew around a collegiate church on the <b>Lahn</b> and received town privileges from Frederick Barbarossa, being treated as an imperial city from about 1180.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar">Free Imperial City of Wetzlar</a></li></ul></section>
